/* book: lead: Emotional arousal changes the skin, breathing and pulse. A polygraph records these channels together on one time base and marks the instant each question is asked. The skin channel is electrodermal activity: basal skin resistance (BSR) is the resting level, and the galvanic skin response (GSR) is the change after a stimulus. Clinics use GSR in biofeedback and autonomic testing; forensic teams use  */
/* book Figure 6.7.1: A polygraph chart with respiration, pulse and skin resistance. After the question, skin resistance stays at the 150 kΩ basal value for a latency of about 1–3 s, falls to 120 kΩ (a 30 kΩ or 20 % fall) and recovers slowly. As conductance the same response is 6.67 to 8.33 µS, a 25 % rise. */
