/* book exam traps: Writing that skin resistance rises with arousal. Sweat raises conductance, so resistance falls; BSR is the resting level and the response is the change from it. | Calling the GSR response the baseline. BSR is the resting value; GSR is the transient fall after a stimulus. | Expecting an immediate response. Latency is about 1 to 3 s, and recovery is slower; a marker placed too early misreads latency. | Reading the voltage trace as resistance without inverting. In a constant-current meter a lower voltage means lower resistance, so the resistance plot is upside down. | Saying the polygraph detects lies. It records arousal; the interpretation is separate and, in Indian courts, corroborative only. */
