/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Intra-ocular pressure (IOP) is the pressure of the aqueous humour inside the eyeball.
   - Indentation tonometry uses a Schiotz tonometer.
   - Applanation tonometry flattens a small area.
   - P is pressure in pascals (Pa); F is force in newtons (N); A is flattened area in square metres (m²).
   - A Goldmann tonometer uses a prism on the slit lamp.
   - Non-contact tonometry uses a short air puff.
   - Calibration checks: zero the instrument, check the prism or probe, and test against a known calibration verifier.
   - The slit lamp is a biomicroscope: a microscope and a lamp on one movable base.
   - With wide diffuse light you scan lids, conjunctiva, cornea, iris and lens.
   - The slit lamp is a platform, not one test.
*/
