/* book exam traps: mmHg is not Pa: 1 mmHg = 133.3 Pa. Convert before comparing with a pascal answer, and keep mN to N straight (20 mN = 0.020 N). | Imbert-Fick assumes a thin, dry, flexible cornea. The tear film pulls the prism in, so the Goldmann design fixes the area and relies on prism calibration. | A Schiotz scale reading is not mmHg. The conversion table depends on the weight used and the instrument. | Refraction is not visual acuity. A 6/6 eye with glasses can still have a -2.00 D prescription. | The slit lamp is not a tonometer. It is a viewing platform; pressure comes from a tonometer, often mounted on it. */
